-- Plan 0.36 north-star test (EFFECTFUL — THE CROWN CASE). Functor shape #5
-- (leaf binary tree, TWO recursive positions), effect-emitting: emit each
-- leaf's Int. Combines the case the legacy codegen could not do (two recursive
-- children) with effect ORDERING across them.
--
-- THIS IS THE ONLY TEST IN THE TREE THAT CAN SEE THE FOLD'S ORDER. A functor
-- with ONE recursive position cannot order anything — `layer5-cata-list-emit`
-- emits [5, 3] whichever way the fold runs. Two positions can, and D221 is what
-- this test found once it was made to assert the trace: the machine folded
-- RIGHT-to-left, emitting [2, 40], while `seqF (G ⊗ H)` specifies left-first.
--
-- D220: it could not find that before, because it ended
-- `main = let r = emitTree t in exit@S 7` — a SUSPENSION, bound and never
-- forced. It emitted nothing and compiled byte-identical to a bare
-- `main = exit@S 7`. The effect must sit on `main`'s composition chain, and a
-- top-level def is required to put it there (plan 0.94).
--
-- Expected: exit code 7, trace [40, 2] — LEFT leaf first.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

-- node(leaf 40, leaf 2)
t : Mu (K Int + (Id * Id))
t = In (inr (In (inl 40), In (inl 2)))

-- algebra : (Int + (Unit * Unit)) ->[eff] Unit  (leaf: emit it; node: unit)
emitTree : Eff (Mu (K Int + (Id * Id))) Unit
emitTree = cata (case emit@E terminal)

-- The fold, as a morphism `main` can compose with.
runTree : IO Unit
runTree = emitTree t

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose (\_ -> 7) runTree)
